ROWS = [
dict(id="T159", d="RMT", t="Local universality of the two-dimensional Coulomb gas at every temperature",
 s="Let x_1, …, x_N ∈ R² have joint density proportional to exp(−β H_N(x)), where H_N(x) = Σ_{i<j} −log|x_i − x_j| + N Σ_i V(x_i), β > 0 is fixed and V is smooth and grows fast enough at infinity. Let z be an interior point of the support of the equilibrium measure at which its density m(z) is positive. Then the rescaled configuration {√(N m(z)) (x_i − z)} converges in law as N → ∞, and the limit point process depends on β alone, not on V or on z.",
 c="At β = 2 the gas is the eigenvalue law of the random normal matrix model, a determinantal process, and local universality is proved there (Ameur–Hedenmalm–Makarov, Duke Math. J. 159 (2011); Hedenmalm–Wennman, Acta Math. 227 (2021); both as cited by Serfaty): the bulk limit is the infinite Ginibre point process scaled to unit intensity. For V(x) = |x|²/2 the gas at β = 2 is the Ginibre ensemble itself, the eigenvalues of an N × N matrix with independent complex Gaussian entries of variance 1/N. For β ≠ 2 no limit has been identified, and convergence along the full sequence, rather than along subsequences, is part of the question. The analogous statement for one-dimensional β-ensembles is a theorem (Bourgade–Erdős–Yau, Duke Math. J. 163 (2014) 1127–1190). Crystallisation of the gas at low temperature is a separate question (compare T044).",
 src="S. Serfaty, Lectures on Coulomb and Riesz gases, arXiv:2407.21194 (2024)",
 loc="Serfaty, arXiv:2407.21194, Section 1.3 'Questions', paragraph 'Universality, phase portrait, phase transitions', printed p. 17: that the local arrangement of the particles does not depend on V 'has been proven only in the one-dimensional logarithmic case of β-ensembles …, and in the two-dimensional Coulomb case only for β = 2'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.21194",
 why="The two-dimensional one-component plasma is the basic classical model of a charged gas, and at β = 2 it is the eigenvalue process of non-Hermitian random matrices. Local universality would say that its microscopic structure at a given temperature is the same at every bulk point and for every confining potential, the planar counterpart of bulk universality for one-dimensional β-ensembles.",
 st="Proved at β = 2 only. For every β > 0 there are a large deviation principle for the empirical field (Leblé–Serfaty, Invent. Math. 210 (2017) 645–757), local laws down to the microscopic scale with the existence of limit point processes along subsequences, generalising the Ginibre process (Armstrong–Serfaty, arXiv:1906.09848), and Gaussian fluctuations of smooth linear statistics, the electrostatic potential converging to a Gaussian free field (Leblé–Serfaty, arXiv:1609.08088; Bauerschmidt–Bourgade–Nikula–Yau, arXiv:1609.08582). Recent exact results are again at β = 2 (Nagao, arXiv:2603.26280, correlation functions on an annulus; Ma–Meng, arXiv:2608.27880, edge statistics for radial potentials). Uniqueness of the limit and its independence of V and z are open for every β ≠ 2. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): posed prominently in Serfaty's lecture notes (Courant, ENS and the 2024 Saint-Flour school) and well known among workers on Coulomb gases and non-Hermitian random matrices, less so beyond."),
dict(id="T160", d="PERC", t="Existence of Kesten's incipient infinite cluster in three dimensions",
 s="For critical Bernoulli bond percolation on Z³, with B_n = [−n, n]³, the conditional laws P_{p_c}( · | 0 ↔ ∂B_n) converge as n → ∞, on events that depend on finitely many edges, to a limiting law: the incipient infinite cluster.",
 c="Kesten defined the incipient infinite cluster in the plane both by this limit and as the limit of P_p( · | 0 ↔ ∞) as p ↓ p_c, and proved that the two limits exist and agree. If θ(p_c) > 0 on Z³ the limit above exists trivially and equals P_{p_c}( · | 0 ↔ ∞); the content of the question is the case θ(p_c) = 0, which is itself open (T001). The three-dimensional critical exponents are T087, and random walk on the planar incipient cluster is T102.",
 src="H. Kesten, The incipient infinite cluster in two-dimensional percolation, Probab. Theory Related Fields 73 (1986) 369–394; D. Basu and A. Sapozhnikov, Kesten's incipient infinite cluster and quasi-multiplicativity of crossing probabilities, arXiv:1603.06884",
 loc="Basu–Sapozhnikov, arXiv:1603.06884v2, p. 2: 'the existence of Kesten's IIC on Z^d for d ≥ 3 is still an open problem'; Theorem 1.1 (existence under (A1), at most one infinite cluster at every p, and (A2), quasi-multiplicativity of crossing probabilities near p_c) and Comment 5 on p. 3 ((A2) is believed to hold on Z^d for d < 6 and to fail for d > 6)",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.06884",
 why="The incipient infinite cluster is critical percolation seen from a typical point of a large cluster. Random walk on it is the model of anomalous diffusion at criticality (the Alexander–Orbach question), and its existence is a first step towards any description of the large critical clusters in three dimensions, where neither conformal invariance nor the lace expansion is available.",
 st="Known in two dimensions (Kesten 1986; further constructions by Járai, Ann. Probab. 31 (2003); a short quantitative construction by Hillairet, arXiv:2509.18852) and in high dimensions through the lace expansion (van der Hofstad–Járai, J. Stat. Phys. 114 (2004); Heydenreich–van der Hofstad–Hulshof, J. Stat. Phys. 155 (2014), arXiv:1108.4325; Chatterjee–Chinmay–Hanson–Sosoe, arXiv:2502.10882; a bi-infinite version by Cabezas–Fribergh–Heydenreich–Járai, arXiv:2506.06559). Basu–Sapozhnikov (arXiv:1603.06884) prove existence on slabs Z² × {0, …, k}^{d−2}, and on any bounded-degree graph with at most one infinite cluster, which holds on Z³, on which crossing probabilities are quasi-multiplicative; they expect quasi-multiplicativity on Z^d for d < 6, but it is not proved for Z³. Analogues are known for the FK–Ising model on Z^d, d ≥ 3 (Panis, arXiv:2406.15243) and for the metric-graph Gaussian free field (Cai–Ding, arXiv:2412.05709). Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a standard open problem of three-dimensional critical percolation, ranked high by the w673 audit; it sits next to T001 and T087."),
dict(id="T161", d="TREE", t="The scaling limit of the three-dimensional uniform spanning tree",
 s="Let U be the uniform spanning tree of Z³ and β ≈ 1.624 the growth exponent of three-dimensional loop-erased random walk. Show that the rescaled trees (U, δ^β d_U, δ³ μ_U, δ φ_U, ρ_U), with d_U the intrinsic metric, μ_U the counting measure, φ_U the embedding into R³ and ρ_U the root at the origin, converge in law as δ → 0 to a single limit, in the Gromov–Hausdorff-type topology on measured, rooted, spatial trees used by Angel, Croydon, Hernandez-Torres and Shiraishi.",
 c="Tightness, and convergence along one particular sequence of scales, are known; what is missing is convergence along all scales, that is, uniqueness of the subsequential limits. The growth exponent β itself is the subject of T103. Convergence statements for three-dimensional loop-erased random walk do not transfer to the whole tree without a joint argument for the branches built by Wilson's algorithm.",
 src="O. Angel, D. A. Croydon, S. Hernandez-Torres and D. Shiraishi, Scaling limits of the three-dimensional uniform spanning tree and associated random walk, Ann. Probab. 49 (2021) 3032–3105, arXiv:2003.09055",
 loc="Angel–Croydon–Hernandez-Torres–Shiraishi, arXiv:2003.09055, Section 1.2 ('uniqueness of the scaling limit is as yet unproved') and the definition (1) in Section 1.1 (read by the w673 audit)",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.09055",
 why="In two dimensions the uniform spanning tree converges to an object described by SLE(8) and SLE(2) (Lawler–Schramm–Werner). In three dimensions there is no conformal invariance, and the tree is one of the few three-dimensional critical models where a full scaling limit is within reach, through Kozma's scaling limit of loop-erased random walk and Wilson's algorithm; it would give the first scaling limit of a critical spatial tree in three dimensions.",
 st="Tightness and convergence along a particular sequence of scales, with Hausdorff dimension 3/β for the intrinsic metric of any limit, and the walk dimension, spectral dimension and heat-kernel estimates for the limiting diffusion (Angel–Croydon–Hernandez-Torres–Shiraishi, arXiv:2003.09055). Li, Liu and Shiraishi (arXiv:2605.19419, May 2026) obtain sharp tail exponents for the past of the origin and apply them to three-dimensional sandpile avalanches; they do not address uniqueness of the limit. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a precise question from a major paper, well known to people working on three-dimensional loop-erased walk and spanning trees, less so beyond; a neighbour of T103.",
 x="REPORTED SOLVED: S. Hernández-Torres, X. Li and D. Shiraishi, Sharp one-point estimates and Minkowski content for the scaling limit of three-dimensional loop-erased random walk, arXiv:2403.07256v2 (21 Dec 2025), Remark 1.10(1), p. 9: with their Theorems 1.3, 1.7 and 1.8, 'the convergence of the scaling limit of the UST on Z3 now holds for arbitrary scaling', in the Gromov–Hausdorff–Prohorov topology with the locally uniform topology for the embedding used by Angel–Croydon–Hernández-Torres–Shiraishi. The restriction to dyadic scales in that paper came from its loop-erased random walk inputs (Remark 1.2 there). The value of the growth exponent stays open (T103)."),
dict(id="T162", d="RG", t="The upper tail problem for subgraph counts (the 'infamous upper tail')",
 s="For a fixed graph H with e_H ≥ 1 edges let X_H be the number of copies of H in the binomial random graph G(n, p), and let m_H be the maximum of e_F/v_F over the subgraphs F of H. Determine, for every H, every fixed ε > 0 and every p = p(n) with n^{−1/m_H} ≪ p and (1 + ε) p^{e_H} ≤ 1, the order of magnitude of −log P(X_H ≥ (1 + ε) E X_H), and where possible its asymptotics.",
 c="The first condition puts p above the threshold for the appearance of H; the second excludes p so close to 1 that (1 + ε) E X_H exceeds the number of copies of H in K_n, where the probability is 0. Janson, Oleszkiewicz and Ruciński determined −log P up to a factor of order log(1/p) for every H, which settles constant p. DeMarco and Kahn (2011) conjectured a formula for the order of magnitude for every H, and Šileikis and Warnke disproved it near the appearance threshold with an infinite family of balanced graphs (arXiv:1809.09595). For p → 0 one also asks when log P is asymptotic to the solution of a mean-field variational problem, and which structures (planted cliques or hubs) a graph conditioned on the upper tail contains.",
 src="S. Janson and A. Ruciński, The infamous upper tail, Random Structures Algorithms 20 (2002) 317–342; S. Janson, K. Oleszkiewicz and A. Ruciński, Upper tails for subgraph counts in random graphs, Israel J. Math. 142 (2004) 61–92",
 loc="Šileikis–Warnke, arXiv:1809.09595, Problem 1 on p. 1 (the order of magnitude for fixed ε and arbitrary p; abstract: 'this so-called infamous upper tail problem remains a challenging testbed for concentration inequalities') and Conjecture 1 on p. 2 (the DeMarco–Kahn formula for n^{−1/m_H} < p ≤ (1 + ε)^{−1/e_H}, refuted by their Theorem 1)",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.09595",
 why="It is the standard test of concentration inequalities for sums of dependent indicators. The lower tail is governed by Janson's inequality, but the upper tail of even the triangle count resisted martingale and polynomial-concentration methods for years, and its resolution for cliques came from the nonlinear large deviation theory and the entropic-stability method developed largely for this problem.",
 st="Solved for cliques, and for arithmetic progressions of fixed length in random subsets of the integers, by the entropic-stability method, with a short solution for triangles when n^{−1} log n ≪ p ≪ 1 (Harel–Mousset–Samotij, arXiv:1904.08212), after work of Chatterjee and of DeMarco–Kahn on triangles (Random Structures Algorithms 40 (2012)) and the nonlinear large deviation theory of Chatterjee–Dembo (Adv. Math. 299 (2016)) and Eldan (Geom. Funct. Anal. 28 (2018)), refined by Cook–Dembo (Adv. Math. 373 (2020)) and Augeri (Ann. Probab. 48 (2020)). Progress for regular H (Bhattacharya–Ganguly–Lubetzky–Zhao, Adv. Math. 319 (2017); Harel–Mousset–Samotij); stars in the sparse range (Akhmejanova–Šileikis, arXiv:2501.03404); irregular H for n^{−1/Δ} ≪ p ≪ 1 and the Poisson range for strictly balanced H, with the intermediate range settled for stars (Basak–Karmakar, arXiv:2503.05311, v4 September 2026). Hypergraph versions are also studied (Cook–Nguyen, arXiv:2509.26569). The order of magnitude for general H over all p has been open since the DeMarco–Kahn formula failed. Open.",
 n="Fame: the name 'infamous upper tail' is standard in probabilistic combinatorics; the problem is well known there."),
dict(id="T163", d="GF", t="Gaussian free field fluctuations of lozenge tilings of general domains (Kenyon–Okounkov)",
 s="Let D be a bounded simply connected planar domain with boundary height data h: ∂D → R that admit a limit shape. Let D_N be tileable domains of the triangular lattice such that N^{−1} D_N approximates D, let H_N be the integer-valued height function of a uniformly random lozenge tiling of D_N, whose rescaled boundary values N^{−1} H_N(N ·) converge to h, and let L be the liquid region of the limit shape. Then the centred height H_N(N u) − E H_N(N u), u ∈ L, converges to π^{−1/2} times the Gaussian free field on L with Dirichlet boundary conditions in the Kenyon–Okounkov complex structure, that is, the pull-back by a uniformising map φ: L → C₊ of the field on the upper half-plane with covariance (2π)^{−1} log|(z − w̄)/(z − w)|.",
 c="Convergence may be asked in the sense of moments at distinct points, as in the polygonal case, or as random distributions. If L has several components, the Dirichlet field on L is a family of independent fields on the components (Huang, Remark 2.7, expects exactly this). Points at which the boundary data force the height are frozen, and the tiling then splits into separate domains. On non-simply-connected domains the prediction fails as stated: a discrete Gaussian component appears (Borot–Gorin–Guionnet, arXiv:2601.16377). Laslier (arXiv:2102.05544) shows that for every limit shape without frozen regions some sequence of domains has that limit shape and fluctuations converging to a variant of the Gaussian free field; the question is for a given domain. The six-vertex analogue is T063.",
 src="R. Kenyon and A. Okounkov, Limit shapes and the complex Burgers equation, Acta Math. 199 (2007) 263–302, arXiv:math-ph/0507007; R. Kenyon, Height fluctuations in the honeycomb dimer model, Comm. Math. Phys. 281 (2008) 675–709",
 loc="Huang, arXiv:2609.04789, Conjecture 2.3 on p. 12 (the prediction, attributed to Kenyon–Okounkov 2007 and Kenyon 2008), with the normalisation of (2.13)–(2.15) and Theorem 2.8 on pp. 13–14 (the polygonal case, under Assumption 2.6)",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.04789",
 why="Uniform lozenge tilings, equivalently dimers on the hexagonal lattice or stepped surfaces, are the basic exactly solvable model of random surfaces. The prediction is that their fluctuations are universal, a Gaussian free field whose conformal structure is set by the limit shape, and it is the model case of the expected Gaussian free field behaviour of random height functions.",
 st="Proved for a class of domains without frozen regions (Kenyon, Comm. Math. Phys. 281 (2008)), for families of polygons by Petrov (Ann. Probab. 43 (2015) 1–43), Bufetov–Gorin (Adv. Math. 338 (2018)) and Huang (arXiv:2011.01751, polygons with one horizontal upper boundary edge), and, in the sense of moments at distinct points, for the domains N P with P any simply connected polygon whose 3d sides cycle through the three lattice directions, provided the liquid region is non-empty and connected and the boundary data force the height at no point (Huang, arXiv:2609.04789, 4 Sep 2026, Theorem 2.8 under Assumption 2.6). Domains that are not polygons, general approximating sequences, and polygons with a disconnected liquid region or a non-empty forced set are not covered. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline-to-strong (my judgment): a central prediction of integrable probability. Its polygonal case was proved in September 2026; the row keeps the general domain, which is how the prediction was made."),
dict(id="T164", d="DYN", t="Ibragimov's conjecture: the central limit theorem for φ-mixing sequences",
 s="Let (X_k, k ∈ Z) be a strictly stationary sequence that is φ-mixing (uniformly mixing), with E X_0² < ∞ and Var(X_1 + … + X_n) → ∞. Then (S_n − E S_n)/√Var(S_n) converges in law to the standard normal distribution, where S_n = X_1 + … + X_n.",
 c="Here φ(n) is the supremum of |P(B | A) − P(B)| over events A with P(A) > 0 measurable with respect to the past up to time 0 and events B measurable with respect to the future from time n, and φ-mixing means φ(n) → 0, with no rate. Iosifescu conjectured that under the same hypotheses the weak invariance principle holds. Under the weaker strong (α-) mixing condition the central limit theorem can fail: Herrndorf constructed strictly stationary, strongly mixing sequences with finite second moments that do not satisfy it (Stationary strongly mixing sequences not satisfying the central limit theorem, Ann. Probab. 11 (1983) 809–813).",
 src="I. A. Ibragimov and Yu. V. Linnik, Independent and Stationary Sequences of Random Variables, Wolters-Noordhoff, Groningen (1971); R. C. Bradley, Basic properties of strong mixing conditions. A survey and some open questions, Probab. Surveys 2 (2005) 107–144, arXiv:math/0511078",
 loc="Bradley, arXiv:math/0511078, Section 2.3, p. 114 of the journal pagination (read 14 Oct): 'These conjectures remain unsolved.'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0511078",
 why="φ-mixing is one of the classical mixing conditions under which limit theorems for dependent sequences are proved. The conjecture asks whether uniform mixing alone, with no rate of mixing and no growth condition beyond Var S_n → ∞, already gives the central limit theorem, as finite variance does for independent sequences. It is tied to an open question in the hierarchy of mixing conditions: if φ-mixing implied ρ*-mixing (Bradley's Question 2), the conjecture would follow from known central limit theorems under ρ*-mixing.",
 st="Peligrad (Ann. Probab. 13 (1985) 1304–1313, Corollary 2.2) proved the central limit theorem and the invariance principle under the stronger hypothesis lim inf Var(S_n)/n > 0. Bradley's survey records both conjectures as unsolved and connects them with his Question 2; Bradley (1999, 'Can a theorem of Csáki and Fischer provide a key to Ibragimov's conjecture?', Bolyai Soc. Math. Stud. 9, 11–42) examines one approach. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a classical conjecture of the limit theory of dependent sequences, well known to specialists in mixing conditions, less so elsewhere.",
 x="REPORTED DISPROVED: a strictly stationary, centred, φ-mixing sequence with finite second moments and Var(S_n) → ∞ whose normalised sums S_n/√Var(S_n) converge to 0 in probability along a subsequence, with a Lean 4 proof of the negation of the Formal Conjectures statement (T. Adamczewski, proof written by the AI system GPT-6 Astra, https://github.com/tadamcz/phi-mixing-clt, September 2026, unrefereed). The submitted statement and definitions are identical to the challenge file, which states the conjecture as here (the φ coefficient over all past and future σ-fields of the sequence, centring, E X_0² < ∞, Var(S_n) → ∞, weak convergence to N(0, 1)) for a sequence indexed by N; nothing is lost, since a strictly stationary sequence indexed by N extends to one indexed by Z with the same finite-dimensional laws and the same φ coefficients; the recorded comparator run reports that Lean's default kernel accepts the disproof with only the standard axioms. If the proof stands it also refutes Iosifescu's invariance principle. Not yet independently rebuilt; restore the row if it fails."),
dict(id="T165", d="Q", t="Positive recurrence of semimartingale reflecting Brownian motion in four and more dimensions",
 s="Let Z be a semimartingale reflecting Brownian motion (SRBM) in the orthant R^d_+ with drift θ, nonsingular covariance matrix Σ and reflection matrix R (completely-S, so that Z exists and is unique in law). Find necessary and sufficient conditions on (θ, Σ, R) for Z to be positive recurrent, that is, for the expected time to hit any open neighbourhood of the origin to be finite from every starting state, in every dimension d ≥ 4.",
 c="Sufficient conditions exist in every dimension, for example the fluid criterion of Dupuis and Williams (every path of the associated fluid model is attracted to the origin). In d = 3 this criterion is also necessary, but Bramson's examples in d = 6 show that it is not necessary in high dimensions, so a different kind of condition is needed.",
 src="M. Bramson, J. G. Dai and J. M. Harrison, Positive recurrence of reflecting Brownian motion in three dimensions, Ann. Appl. Probab. 20 (2010) 753–783, arXiv:1009.5746",
 loc="Bramson–Dai–Harrison, arXiv:1009.5746, abstract: 'The problem of recurrence classification for SRBMs in four and higher dimensions remains open.'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1009.5746",
 why="SRBMs are the heavy-traffic diffusion limits of open multiclass queueing networks, and their positive recurrence is the diffusion counterpart of network stability. The instability examples for multiclass networks (Rybko–Stolyar, Bramson's FIFO networks) show that the usual traffic condition is not enough, and the SRBM question asks for the right condition in the limit.",
 st="Settled for d = 2, and for d = 3 by Bramson, Dai and Harrison, who, building on El Kharroubi, Ben Tahar and Yaacoubi (Stochastics Stochastics Rep. 68 (2000); Math. Methods Oper. Res. 56 (2002)), give necessary and sufficient conditions that are simple to check; in d = 3 positive recurrence holds if and only if every fluid path is attracted to the origin. In d = 6 Bramson (arXiv:1010.1751) gives positive recurrent examples with a divergent linear fluid path. Recent work concerns stationary distributions in low dimension (Flin, arXiv:2604.27951, the half-plane with two-sided reflection), not the classification. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a well-known question in applied probability and queueing theory, less known outside it."),
dict(id='T166',
 x='Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Capacity of the Gaussian interference channel: information theory.',
 d='STAT',
 t='The capacity region of the two-user Gaussian interference channel',
 s='For the two-user Gaussian interference channel Y_1 = X_1 + √a X_2 + Z_1, Y_2 = √b X_1 + X_2 + Z_2, with independent standard Gaussian noise and average power constraints P_1, P_2, determine the capacity region, in particular for weak interference (a, b < 1) and mixed interference (a < 1 ≤ b).',
 c='The question is for a computable, single-letter description; the multi-letter Han–Kobayashi region is known to be the capacity region (Liu, arXiv:2209.00163, p. 2). A long-standing expectation is that the Han–Kobayashi scheme, in which each receiver decodes part of the interfering message and treats the rest as noise, achieves capacity with suitable Gaussian inputs. Unrefereed preprints claim the capacity region of the one-sided channel (Vaezi–Poor, arXiv:1509.02953, 2015), a solution for weak interference (Khandani, arXiv:2011.12981 and arXiv:2501.14941, v13 of 24 Sep 2026) and a finite characterisation of general multiuser capacity regions with the interference channel as the main example (Cioffi, arXiv:2609.17212, 15 Sep 2026); later work, for example Liu (arXiv:2209.00163), still treats even the one-sided (Z) channel as open.',
 src='T. S. Han and K. Kobayashi, A new achievable rate region for the interference channel, IEEE Trans. Inform. Theory 27 (1981) 49–60; R. Etkin, D. Tse and H. Wang, Gaussian interference channel capacity to within one bit, IEEE Trans. Inform. Theory 54 (2008) 5534–5562',
 loc="Liu, arXiv:2209.00163, Section I, pp. 1–2: the interference channel's 'single-letter rate region has remained open after decades of efforts', and it is unknown whether Gaussian inputs are optimal for the Han–Kobayashi bound on the Gaussian Z-interference channel, which would resolve 'the longstanding open problem about the rate region'",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.00163',
 why='It is the simplest network in which two independent transmissions interfere, the basic model for wireless systems with uncoordinated users, and one of the oldest open problems of network information theory; the probabilistic content is an optimisation of mutual information over input distributions that is not known to be attained by Gaussian inputs.',
 st='Known: strong and very strong interference (Carleial, IEEE Trans. Inform. Theory 21 (1975); Sato and Han–Kobayashi, IEEE Trans. Inform. Theory 27 (1981)); the sum capacity in a noisy-interference part of the weak regime, where treating interference as noise is optimal (Shang–Kramer–Chen, Motahari–Khandani, Annapureddy–Veeravalli, all IEEE Trans. Inform. Theory 55 (2009)); the whole region to within one bit (Etkin–Tse–Wang 2008). Temporally correlated Gaussian inputs do not enlarge the Han–Kobayashi region with Gaussian inputs (Nair–Ng, IEEE Trans. Inform. Theory 65 (2019) 1372–1374). For the Gaussian Z-interference channel, Costa, Nair, Ng and Wang conjectured that a Gaussian stationary point is the global optimiser of an inequality whose truth would make the Han–Kobayashi region with Gaussian inputs optimal there; Liu (arXiv:2209.00163) gave counterexamples in some regimes, which make Gaussian inputs suboptimal for the Han–Kobayashi region under constant power control, showed that with variable power control the Gaussian optimisers lie in the regime where the stationary point is stable, and proposed an amended conjecture. Local optimality of Gaussian inputs for the Han–Kobayashi bound on the Z-channel is studied by Nair and Zhao (ISIT 2026). Open.',
 n='Fame: a famous open problem of information theory, included for its probabilistic content (the optimisation of mutual information over input laws); information theory is kept to at most three rows.'),
]
